¶
'''acknowledgement''', ''n.'' See ''acknowledgment.''

'''acknowledger''' (ak-nol′e-jėr), ''n.'' One who acknowledges.

'''acknowledgment''' (ak-nol′ej-ment), ''n.'' '''1.''' An admission or profession of knowledge or apprehension; a recognition of the existence or truth of anything: as, the ''acknowledgment'' of a sovereign power, or of a debt.
{{smaller block|Immediately upon the ''acknowledgment'' of the Christian faith, the eunuch was baptized by Philip. {{right|''Hooker.''}}}}
'''2.''' An expression or manifestation of perception or appreciation; recognition, avowal, or confession: as, an ''acknowledgment'' of kindness or of one’s wrong-doing.
{{smaller block|<poem>With this ''acknowledgment'',
That God fought for us. ''Shak.'', Hen. V., iv. 8.</poem>}}
'''3.''' Something given or done in return for a favor. ''Smollett.''—'''4.''' In ''law'': (''a'') The certificate of a public officer that an instrument was acknowledged before him by the person who executed it. (''b'') The act of so acknowledging execution.—'''5.''' In ''com.'', a receipt.

Also spelled ''acknowledgement.''

{{smaller|'''Acknowledgment money''', in England, money paid according to the customs of some manors by copyhold ten-}}

